// PAIR 2 of 3, root bck.B — the LEGAL twin, ONE TOKEN from
// fail/bc_slicearr_suffix_moved_twice: the second arm is `[w, ..]`, index 0,
// where the first moved index N-sc+j = 2. Disjoint sub-places of one array stay
// usable, which is the whole point of refining the place at all — and it is what
// separates the landed arm from the coarse `slicetype` spelling, which consumes
// the whole array on the first element binding and refuses this too.
package bc_slicearr_suffix_prefix_twin;

// ⚠ THE PAYLOAD IS A `Drop` STRUCT WITH AN i64 FIELD AND THE BINDING IS NEVER
// READ, for a reason that is not this arm's: on this tree, READING a field of
// an array-pattern element binding is a CODEGEN defect. `[_, _, x] => x.n`
// SEGFAULTS and `[ref x, _, _] => x.n` returns garbage, while `a[2u64].n`
// returns 3 — measured 2026-08-30, unrelated to borrow checking, no ledger row.
// A `[String; 3]` with an element moved out ABORTS at runtime for a second
// reason: drop elaboration drops both the moved-out element and the array. The
// measured counter-examples for the two declined spellings are the `[String; 3]`
// twins in the round record; these fixtures pin the SHAPE as legal, admitted,
// and running.
struct P { n: i64 }
impl Drop for P { fn drop(self: &mut P) {} }

fn arr() -> [P; 3] {
    return [P { n: 1i64 }, P { n: 2i64 }, P { n: 3i64 }];
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: [P; 3] = arr();
    match a {
        [.., z] => { }
    }
    match a {
        [w, ..] => { }
    }
    return 0i32;
}
